Introduction
What is it?
• A wearable device or app that records
activity and body signals
• Tracks steps, distance, calories, heart rate
and sleep
• Syncs to a phone app for charts and
coaching
Why it matters
• Regular exercise helps prevent heart
disease and diabetes
• WHO advises 150+ minutes of moderate
activity weekly
• Trackers make daily habits visible and
measurable
2. History and Evolution
Early days
• 1960s: step-counting pedometers, "10,000
steps" idea
• 1980s–90s: electronic pedometers, chest-
strap heart monitors
• 2009: Fitbit clip-on tracker with online
dashboard
Modern era
• 2015: Apple Watch merges tracking with
smartwatch
• Budget bands from Xiaomi widen access
• Today: ECG, blood oxygen, smart rings
(Oura), AI insights
3. Types and Technology
Types of trackers
• Wrist bands and smartwatches
• Sports and multisport watches
• Smart rings and chest straps
• Smartphone apps
Key sensors
• Accelerometer: steps and movement
• Optical PPG: heart rate and blood oxygen
• GPS: distance, pace and routes
• Barometer, temperature and ECG sensors
4. Features and Benefits
Key features
• Steps, workouts, active minutes
• Heart rate, HRV, blood oxygen, ECG
• Sleep scores and stress tools
• Goals, reminders and social challenges
Benefits
• Higher awareness and motivation
• Long-term progress tracking
• Early warning of irregular heart rhythm
• Better sleep and lifestyle habits
• Supports rehab and remote care
5. Limitations and Privacy
Limitations
• Readings are estimates, not lab
measurements
• Skin tone, tattoos and motion affect
optical sensors
• Calorie and sleep-stage figures are
approximate
• Can cause anxiety; many users stop using
them
Privacy and security
• Collect location, biometric and sleep data
• Risks: breaches, third-party sharing, public
maps
• Use strong passwords and two-factor login
• Review permissions and privacy settings
6. Applications and Future Trends
Applications
• Personal fitness and weight goals
• Athletic training and recovery
• Remote patient monitoring
• Elderly care with fall detection
• Corporate wellness programmes
Future trends
• AI-driven personal health coaching
• Non-invasive glucose and blood-pressure
sensing
• Rings, patches and smart clothing
• Links to healthcare records
• Sustainable, longer-lasting devices
Conclusion
• Trackers turn daily behaviour into useful data and motivate healthier
habits
• Their measurements are estimates: watch trends, not single numbers
• Protect your data and use trackers alongside professional medical advice
• AI and new sensors will make them more capable in the years ahead
